//? Title: Compare joint distributions with energy distance
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Compare two groups with the same marginal values

Both groups have the same values on each coordinate. Group A pairs
the coordinates with the same sign, and group B pairs them with
opposite signs. The energy test uses distances in the two-dimensional
space, so it can detect a difference in how the coordinates vary together.

The exact reference assigns eight of the sixteen pooled points to
the first group in every possible way. The simulation samples the
same assignments and applies a plus-one correction to the tail count.

```{cpp, id=pooled-label-energy, results="markdown", file="figures/energy_distance_groups.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>

int main() {
    using namespace dm::stats;
    const std::vector<double> x{-3.4, -3.2, -3, -2.8, 2.8, 3, 3.2, 3.4};
    std::vector<double> opposite;
    dm::linalg::DenseMatrix<double> first(x.size(), 2), second(x.size(), 2);
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < x.size(); ++i) {
        opposite.push_back(-x[i]);
        first(i, 0) = second(i, 0) = x[i];
        first(i, 1) = x[i];
        second(i, 1) = opposite.back();
    }
    const auto exact = energy_distance_test_exact(first, second);
    dm::random::SplitMix64 rng(42);
    const auto simulated = energy_distance_test(first, second, 9999, rng);
    std::println("The empirical energy distance is {:.3f}.", exact.estimate1);
    std::println("");
    std::println("| Reference | Assignments | Upper-tail assignments | p-value |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    std::println("| Exact | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.4f} |",
                 exact.parameter1, exact.parameter2, exact.p_value);
    std::println("| Simulated | {:.0f} | {:.0f} | {:.4f} |",
                 simulated.parameter1, simulated.parameter2, simulated.p_value);

    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    dm::graphics::RPlot::create()
        .points(x, x, "Group A", {37, 99, 235}, 4)
        .points(x, opposite, "Group B", {180, 83, 9}, 4)
        .x_limits(-4.5, 4.5).y_limits(-4.5, 4.5)
        .x_label("First coordinate").y_label("Second coordinate")
        .title("Shared marginal values with different pairings")
        .save_svg("build/energy_distance_groups.svg");
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
The empirical energy distance is 3.294.

